/details></div></div><div class=3D"min-w-0 min-h-0 max-w-fit"><div class=3D=
"article-content max-w-prose mb-20"><p>In order to get enough information f=
rom videos, we often need to extract video frames.
However, we do not want to extract every frame from a video due to:</p><ul>=
<li>information redundancy in successive video frames.</li><li>huge storage=
 space needed by these video frames.</li></ul><p>In this post, I want to sh=
are how to extract <em>key</em> frames from video to reduce the storage,
but still capture enough information from a video.</p><h1 class=3D"relative=
 group">Extract I-frame using ffmpeg<div id=3D"extract-i-frame-using-ffmpeg=
" class=3D"anchor"></div><span class=3D"absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opaci=
ty opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"><a cla=
ss=3D"text-primary-300 dark:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href=3D"https:/=
/jdhao.github.io/2021/12/25/ffmpeg-extract-key-frame-video/#extract-i-frame=
-using-ffmpeg" aria-label=3D"Anchor">#</a></span></h1><p>In video compressi=
on, they use the so-called <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_c=
ompression_picture_types" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer">IPB frames</=
a> to reduce storage space:</p><ul><li>I frames (Intra picture): a complete=
 picture</li><li>P frames (predictive picture): p frames stores difference =
between current frame and previous frame.</li><li>B frames (Bidirectionally=
 predicted picture): b-frame stores difference between current frame and pr=
evious frame and later frame.</li></ul><p>So we can extract I-frames only t=
o get a fair amount of information from the video and reduce the extracted =
frame number.</p><p>To extract I-frames, we can run the following command:<=
/p><div class=3D"highlight-wrapper"><button class=3D"copy-button" type=3D"b=
utton" aria-label=3D"Copy">Copy</button><div class=3D"highlight"><pre tabin=
dex=3D"0" class=3D"chroma"><code class=3D"language-bash" data-lang=3D"bash"=
><span class=3D"line"><span class=3D"cl">ffmpeg -skip_frame nokey -i test.m=
p4 -vsync vfr -frame_pts <span class=3D"nb">true</span> out-%02d.jpeg</span=
></span></code></pre></div></div><ul><li><code>-vsync vfr</code>: discard t=
he unused frames</li><li><code>-frame_pts true</code>: use the frame index =
for image names, otherwise, the index starts from 1 and increments 1 each t=
ime</li></ul><p>We can also use the filter syntax to extract keyframes:</p>=
<div class=3D"highlight-wrapper"><button class=3D"copy-button" type=3D"butt=
on" aria-label=3D"Copy">Copy</button><div class=3D"highlight"><pre tabindex=
=3D"0" class=3D"chroma"><code class=3D"language-bash" data-lang=3D"bash"><s=
pan class=3D"line"><span class=3D"cl">ffmpeg -i test.mp4 -vf <span class=3D=
"s2">"select=3D'eq(pict_type,I)'"</span> -vsync vfr out-%02d.jpeg</span></s=
pan></code></pre></div></div><p>The produced results from the two commands =
are the same.</p><h1 class=3D"relative group">Extract scene-changing frames=
<div id=3D"extract-scene-changing-frames" class=3D"anchor"></div><span clas=
s=3D"absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose gro=
up-hover:opacity-100 select-none"><a class=3D"text-primary-300 dark:text-ne=
utral-700 !no-underline" href=3D"https://jdhao.github.io/2021/12/25/ffmpeg-=
extract-key-frame-video/#extract-scene-changing-frames" aria-label=3D"Ancho=
r">#</a></span></h1><p>If we only want to retain enough info from the video=
, extracting I-frames only may not be enough.
The extracted key frames may still exhibit too much information redundancy.
For example, if you have a slow-changing video, the difference between a fr=
ame and its subsequent frames will be negligible.
To further reduce the number of images generated,
we can also use <a href=3D"http://www.ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-filters.html#select=
_002c-aselect" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer">scene</a> filter to sel=
ect frames that are likely to be a scene-changing frame.</p><p>It works lik=
e this:</p><div class=3D"highlight-wrapper"><button class=3D"copy-button" t=
ype=3D"button" aria-label=3D"Copy">Copy</button><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re tabindex=3D"0" class=3D"chroma"><code class=3D"language-bash" data-lang=
=3D"bash"><span class=3D"line"><span class=3D"cl">ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vf <=
span class=3D"s2">"select=3D'gt(scene,0.4)'"</span> -vsync vfr frame-%2d.jp=
g</span></span></code></pre></div></div><p>The value for <code>scene</code>=
 is between 0 and 1, which measures the pixel level difference between curr=
ent frame and previous frame.
We can tweak this value to generate different number of output images.
Intuitively, the smaller the value, the larger number of images we will ext=
ract.
Typically, a value of 0.3 or 0.4 may be enough.</p><p>In order to extract s=
uitable number of frames from the video, for videos with fast-changing fram=
es,
we should set this value high, and for videos with mostly still frames,
we should set this value low (maybe 0.1 or even less depending on the video=
 content).</p><h1 class=3D"relative group">References<div id=3D"references"=
 class=3D"anchor"></div><span class=3D"absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacit=
